# House rules

Conventions for this workspace. They are Angular <%= angularLine %> defaults plus the few
choices this layout makes for itself.

## Components

- Standalone. No `NgModule` — the CLI has not generated one since v17, and this
  workspace has none.
- `ChangeDetectionStrategy.OnPush` everywhere. With signals it is the only
  strategy that is both correct and cheap.
- `inject()` over constructor parameter injection. It works in field
  initialisers, functions and `inject`-context helpers, and it does not fight
  inheritance.
- Inputs and outputs as `input()`, `input.required()`, `output()` and
  `model()`. Not the decorators.
- Built-in control flow (`@if`, `@for`, `@switch`, `@defer`). `*ngIf` and
  `*ngFor` are legacy here. `@for` requires `track` — use a stable id, not
  `$index`, unless the list is genuinely positional.

## State

- Signals for synchronous state. `computed()` for anything derived: a derived
  value held in a plain field is a value that can go stale.
- `resource()` / `httpResource()` for async reads.
- `effect()` sparingly, and never to write state another signal could compute.
  An effect that assigns to a signal is usually a `computed()` that has not been
  recognised yet.

## Styles

- SCSS. Component styles are scoped by default; leave them that way.
- Consume design tokens from the shared layer rather than restating colours.
  A hex code in a component is a token that does not exist yet.
- Theming is two attributes on `<html>` — `data-theme` and `data-palette` — and
  a stylesheet that declares every combination. Never read the current theme in
  a component to pick a colour: use the token that already means what you want,
  and let it resolve. A component that branches on the theme is a component that
  will be wrong in the next palette.
- `--accent` fills a control and carries `--on-accent`. `--accent-strong` is the
  step that contrasts with the page, and is what accent-coloured *text* uses.
  Swapping them passes review and fails contrast.

## Libraries

Libraries are consumed **from `dist/`**, not from source. `tsconfig.json` maps
each library's import name at its build output, which means the app compiles
against the library's real public API — an export missing from `public-api.ts`
fails at the app, where it should, instead of resolving silently through a
source path.

The cost is one extra step: **`ng serve` does not build workspace libraries.**
A fresh clone must run `npm run build:libs` once. Every app's own `start`,
`watch`, `build`, `test` and `e2e` run it first through their `pre*` hooks
(`angular-capacitor-workspace prepare`), so the npm entry points cover it, from
the root or with `-w`; a bare `ng serve` or `ng test` bypasses them and will
fail to resolve library imports until you have built once. Keep `prepare` as
the hook rather than `npm run build:libs`: the root `npm run build` and
`npm run e2e` prepare once for every project, and `prepare` skips under them.

## Testing

- Unit tests run on Vitest through `@angular/build:unit-test`.
- Component tests for the design system run in **browser mode** against a real
  engine, not jsdom. A component library whose tests never touch a layout
  engine has not been tested.
- End-to-end tests are Playwright, one config per app delegating to
  `playwright.base.ts`.
- Prefer testing behaviour through the rendered DOM over asserting on component
  internals. Queries by role and label survive refactors; queries by CSS class
  do not.

## Scripts

- A project's own scripts (`start`, `watch`, `build`, `test`, `e2e` and their
  `pre*` hooks) go in that project's `package.json`, not the root one. The
  root keeps what concerns the whole workspace and never names a project:
  its `start`, `watch`, `build`, `test` and `e2e` are
  `angular-capacitor-workspace run`, which runs one project named as an
  argument (`npm run build shop`), or with no name, `build`, `test` and `e2e`
  for every project. Any other project script runs with
  `npm run <script> -w <package>`.
- The project runner, the prerender checks, codegen, the contrast check and
  the mobile preflight are `angular-capacitor-workspace` commands, from the
  installed `@angular-capacitor-workspace/cli`, so updating the package
  updates them. Never replace one with a copy in this repository: a copy stops
  getting fixes. What they check is configured in files this workspace owns,
  such as the design system's `contrast.config.mjs`.
- A project's script runs in the project's directory. Reach a root script with
  `--prefix` back to the root (`npm run build:libs --prefix ../../..`) and a
  root file by its relative path.
- Dependencies go in the root `package.json`, never a web project's. A mobile
  shell is the exception: its Capacitor packages and plugins live in its own,
  because Capacitor discovers plugins from there.

## Dependencies

This workspace carries a dependency policy. Before adding a package:

- `npx @angular-capacitor-workspace/cli audit` runs the same gate that ran at
  generation — resolve a lockfile, audit it, fail on anything unaccounted for.
- `npx @angular-capacitor-workspace/cli doctor` reports drift from the current
  policy, and `--fix` applies it.
- Upgrade the generator with `ng update @angular-capacitor-workspace/cli@<%= angularLine %>`,
  then `doctor --fix`. `ng update` runs its migrations and `npm install` does
  not. A migration that finds a file edited since generation leaves it alone and
  prints the change to make by hand. Make that change.
- `allowScripts` in `package.json` is an allowlist: a dependency that wants to
  run an install script needs a deliberate entry. Adding one is a decision, not
  a formality.
